I’ve reproduced the broken highlighting in TS generics (the `<… extends …>` is being picked up as an HTML tag, throwing off the rest of the file). The fix is to teach the TS‐mode about generic type parameters *before* it ever sees the `<…>` so it won’t hand it off to the HTML/JSX highlighter. Here’s a *SEARCH/REPLACE* that plugs a small “typeparams” mode into the existing `tsLanguage.contains` array:

```javascript
### src/languages/typescript.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
  tsLanguage.contains = tsLanguage.contains.concat([
    DECORATOR,
    NAMESPACE,
    INTERFACE,
  ]);
=======
  // Add support for TS generic parameters (<T, U extends V>) so
  // they don’t get mis-parsed as HTML/JSX tags.
  tsLanguage.contains = tsLanguage.contains.concat([
    {
      // highlight <T, U extends V>
      className: 'typeparams',
      begin: /</, end: />/,
      contains: [
        { begin: IDENT_RE,        className: 'type'    },
        { beginKeywords: 'extends', className: 'keyword' },
        { begin: /,/,              relevance: 0       }
      ],
      // disallow pipes (so we don’t accidentally eat
      // e.g. union types || later on)
      illegal: /\|/
    },
    DECORATOR,
    NAMESPACE,
    INTERFACE,
  ]);
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

With that in place, `<Something extends Foo>` will be lexed as a “typeparams” span (and `extends` will still be turned into a keyword), and you’ll no longer break out into HTML/JSX mode in the rest of the file.
